// §9.13: "Verilog-AMS HDL extends the probabilistic distribution functions so
// that they are supported in the analog context." Table 9-10 lists all 17 names
// and every row reads `Yes  Yes` — digital context AND analog context. So this
// file must COMPILE and run green; a compiler that refuses the call is the one
// that is wrong, which is why the expectation is a run and not a reject.
//
// Syntax 9-8 spells the seed as `analog_random_seed ::= ... | [ sign ]
// decimal_number`, so a negative literal is a legal seed and the call must be
// accepted. No equality is claimed between the two calls: the constant seeds an
// INTERNAL seed which "gets updated every time the call to $arandom is made",
// so the second call is free to differ. Only the range of the result is fixed.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE CAN AND CANNOT PROVE. The seed is a CONSTANT, so there is
// no inout to observe — §9.13.1 says a constant seed "does not update the
// parameter value" and the internal seed it creates is not visible from the
// source. §9.13.1 fixes nothing else about the returned value here. The one
// sentence left with content is the width: "the random number returned is a
// 32-bit signed integer; it can be positive or negative." That is the check
// below, and it is the weakest in the
// chapter: it is falsifiable only on a host whose integer type is wider than 32
// bits (VerA carries integers in i64 — see the width note in
// 07_file_open_close.va), and satisfied for free where `integer` is exactly 32.
//
// It is deliberately NOT the chapter's old `($random % 60) is in [-59,59]`.
// §9.13.1 does print that example, but as an assertion it is EMPTY: a % 60 lies
// in [-59,59] for every integer a of every width, including 0, so it cannot fail
// under any implementation. Dressing an identity as a check is worse than
// admitting the clause pins nothing.
//
// The discriminating checks for this family are elsewhere and are cited here so
// the gap is not silent: 32_random.va / 33_arandom.va pin the inout seed rule
// ("a value is passed to the function and a different value is returned") and
// 117_arandom_parameter_seed.va pins the parameter form that must NOT be
// updated. This file's own content is that Syntax 9-8's `[ sign ]
// decimal_number` seed is ACCEPTED at all.
//
//! lrm 9.13.1
`include "check.vh"
module ch09_arandom_negative_seed(p, n);
  inout p, n; electrical p, n;
  integer a, c;
  analog begin
    a = $arandom(-7);
    c = $arandom(-7);
    `CHECKI("Syntax 9-8 admits a signed decimal_number as the seed",
            (a >= -2147483648) && (a <= 2147483647), 1);
    `CHECKI("and so does the second call site",
            (c >= -2147483648) && (c <= 2147483647), 1);
    I(p,n) <+ V(p,n) + a + c;
  end
endmodule
